// Running a query against a database while the program runs.
//
//	match var r = sql.query(engine=sql.Engine.sqlite, source="books.db", text="SELECT id, title FROM books WHERE id = ?", args="[7]", columns=["id", "title"]) {
//	    ok { … json.decode[list[Book]](r) … }
//	    failed { … sql.classify(r).kind … }
//	}
//
// A program rarely writes one: `import db "sql:./sqlc.yaml"` reads the
// queries sqlc analyses and writes a typed function per query.
//
// **The text is the database's own**, with its own placeholders, and the
// arguments and the rows travel as JSON: the arguments an array, the rows an
// array of objects keyed by `columns`, which `json.decode` reads as the
// program's own structs. So each language's runtime is one call with no
// type in it, and a row is checked against its struct by the codec every
// target already holds.
//
// A query waits, as any async function does. The connection to a source is
// opened on its first query and kept for the program's life.
